.TH GP-FORK 1 "2026-10-04" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
gp-fork \- keep a fork of someone else's repository up to date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gp fork sync
[\fB\-b\fR|\fB\-\-base\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.br
.B gp fork
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A clone of a fork has two remotes:
.I origin
(your fork) and
.I upstream
(the repository it was forked from, the name
.B gh repo fork \-\-clone
gives it; otherwise add it with
.BR "git remote add upstream <url>" ).
.PP
.B gp fork sync
catches the fork's base branch up with upstream's, both in your clone and on
origin. It does what
.B git pull upstream <base> && git push origin <base>
does, but from any branch, and it only ever fast-forwards. The local base is
updated wherever it lives: in this worktree, in another worktree, or not
checked out at all. Upstream's tip is then pushed to origin with a plain push,
never forced.
.PP
A fork's base should only follow upstream, so a base with commits of its own
is left as is and reported, here or on origin, and the exit status is 1. So
is a fast-forward that uncommitted changes would overwrite.
.PP
It only touches the base branch. To rebase a feature branch onto the fresh
base, follow it with
.BR "gp sync" ,
which works against origin as usual.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fINAME\fR
Sync
.I NAME
instead of origin's default branch.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Fetch, then print what would happen without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the base is up to date with upstream, both here and on origin; 1 when
something was left as is or a guard failed; 2 for a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gp fork sync
Pull upstream's latest base into your clone and push it to your fork.
.TP
.B gp fork sync && gp sync
Then rebase the current feature branch onto it and push.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gp-sync (1),
.BR gowork (1),
.BR gh-repo-fork (1)
